WHAT: give KCoreAddons a WASI branch in the four places it reaches for a POSIX
      facility wasi-libc does not have -- statfs/statvfs, socketpair,
      struct rlimit, and QProcess.
WHY:  four separate compile failures, none of them related except in cause:
        * io/kfilesystemtype.cpp:243 "use of undeclared identifier
          determineFileSystemTypeImpl". Misleading: the function is not
          missing, it was never DEFINED. The non-Windows half of this file is a
          chain of #if Q_OS_BSD4 / #elif Q_OS_LINUX / #elif Q_OS_AIX... with NO
          trailing #else -- the #else near the bottom belongs to the
          #ifndef Q_OS_WIN at the top -- so an unrecognised UNIX silently
          defines nothing and only the call site complains.
        * util/ksignalhandler.cpp:39 "no member named socketpair in the global
          namespace". wasi-libc hides socketpair behind
          __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream. The whole class is the self-pipe
          trick, and wasip2 has no asynchronous signals to feed it anyway
          (-D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL is stubs), so it degrades exactly as it
          already does on Windows: constructing one works and it never emits.
        * caching/ksdcmapping_p.h:194 "variable has incomplete type struct
          rlimit" / "use of undeclared identifier RLIMIT_MEMLOCK". wasi-libc
          ships a sys/resource.h with rusage and getrusage but no rlimit at
          all. The block is a best-effort mlock() budget used to prove the
          mapping is readable; skipping it loses a diagnostic, not the cache.
        * src/lib/CMakeLists.txt: kprocess.h:14 and ksandbox.h:7 both
          #include <QProcess>, and wk qtbase is FEATURE_process=OFF -- there is
          no fork or exec across a WASI component boundary, and qprocess.h is
          not installed. WASI joins iOS in the exclusion that already exists
          for exactly this reason.
UPSTREAM: the kfilesystemtype hunk is as-is (their missing #else is a real
      portability bug -- any non-listed UNIX hits it). The other three are
      not-as-is: correct here, but upstream would want a configure check
      rather than a Q_OS_WASI test.
diff --git a/src/lib/CMakeLists.txt b/src/lib/CMakeLists.txt
index 00df4a8..61c90c0 100644
--- a/src/lib/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/lib/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -186,7 +186,18 @@ elseif(HAVE_QTDBUS)
     target_sources(KF6CoreAddons PRIVATE util/ksystemclockskewnotifierengine_dbus.cpp)
 endif()
 
-if(NOT IOS)
+# WASI joins iOS here. Both kprocess.h:14 and ksandbox.h:7 do `#include
+# <QProcess>`, and wk's qtbase is FEATURE_process=OFF -- there is no fork and
+# no exec behind a WASI component's boundary, so qprocess.h is not even
+# installed and these two fail at the #include. KProcess is a QProcess
+# subclass, so there is nothing to salvage; KSandbox answers "are we in a
+# Flatpak/Snap?", which for a wk node is a question with a different (and
+# better) answer than either. A node that wants to run another program uses
+# wk:exec, not QProcess -- see plugins/exec-compat.
+# Consequence to know about: KXmlGui's kabstractaboutdialog_p.cpp calls
+# KSandbox::isFlatpak()/isSnap(), so it needs the matching patch. Nothing in
+# KCalc's graph uses KProcess at all.
+if(NOT IOS AND NOT CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
     target_sources(KF6CoreAddons PRIVATE
         io/kprocess.cpp
         util/ksandbox.cpp
diff --git a/src/lib/caching/ksdcmapping_p.h b/src/lib/caching/ksdcmapping_p.h
index b72d36e..04030ce 100644
--- a/src/lib/caching/ksdcmapping_p.h
+++ b/src/lib/caching/ksdcmapping_p.h
@@ -17,7 +17,14 @@
 #include <QtGlobal>
 #include <qplatformdefs.h>
 
+#ifndef Q_OS_WASI
+// wasi-libc ships a sys/resource.h that defines struct rusage and getrusage
+// but NOT struct rlimit, rlim_t or any RLIMIT_* constant -- the upstream block
+// that would define them is inside `#ifdef __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream`.
+// Including it is harmless; relying on rlimit below is not. See the mlock
+// block further down.
 #include <sys/resource.h>
+#endif
 
 #if defined(_POSIX_MAPPED_FILES) && ((_POSIX_MAPPED_FILES == 0) || (_POSIX_MAPPED_FILES >= 200112L))
 #define KSDC_MAPPED_FILES_SUPPORTED 1
@@ -190,6 +197,7 @@ private:
             // with a larger size. If that's the case we need to at least match
             // the size to be able to access every entry, so fixup the mapping.
             if (mapAddress != MAP_FAILED) {
+#ifndef Q_OS_WASI
                 // Successful mmap doesn't actually mean that whole range is readable so ensure it is
                 struct rlimit memlock;
                 if (getrlimit(RLIMIT_MEMLOCK, &memlock) == 0 && memlock.rlim_cur >= 2) {
@@ -207,6 +215,7 @@ private:
                 } else {
                     qCWarning(KCOREADDONS_DEBUG) << "Failed to get RLIMIT_MEMLOCK!";
                 }
+#endif
 
                 SharedMemory *mapped = reinterpret_cast<SharedMemory *>(mapAddress);
 
diff --git a/src/lib/io/kfilesystemtype.cpp b/src/lib/io/kfilesystemtype.cpp
index 49ab7d8..0e9b901 100644
--- a/src/lib/io/kfilesystemtype.cpp
+++ b/src/lib/io/kfilesystemtype.cpp
@@ -208,6 +208,23 @@ static KFileSystemType::Type determineFileSystemTypeImpl(const QByteArray &path)
     }
 }
 
+#elif defined(Q_OS_WASI)
+// A wk node's filesystem is wk's own VFS -- layered, sometimes a guest-served
+// wk:fs provider, sometimes a host bind mount -- and none of statfs(2),
+// statvfs(2) or /proc/mounts exists to ask. Unknown is the honest answer, and
+// it is the same answer the (Q_OS_WIN-side) fallback at the bottom of this
+// file already gives. This branch is needed because the non-Windows side of
+// the #ifndef Q_OS_WIN has NO trailing #else: the `#else` further down belongs
+// to `#ifndef Q_OS_WIN` at the top, so an unrecognised UNIX simply never
+// defines determineFileSystemTypeImpl and the link/compile fails at its one
+// call site.
+
+KFileSystemType::Type determineFileSystemTypeImpl(const QByteArray &path)
+{
+    Q_UNUSED(path);
+    return KFileSystemType::Unknown;
+}
+
 #elif defined(Q_OS_AIX) || defined(Q_OS_HPUX) || defined(Q_OS_QNX) || defined(Q_OS_SCO) || defined(Q_OS_UNIXWARE) || defined(Q_OS_RELIANT)                     \
     || defined(Q_OS_NETBSD)
 #include <sys/statvfs.h>
